SYSTEM PRINCIPLE

Ripple is a multi-package system that builds a persistent memory layer for software development.
All packages MUST operate on a shared canonical data model called RippleCanonicalModel.
No subsystem is allowed to define its own version of truth.

CORE ARCHITECTURE RULES

- All cross-package data MUST conform to RippleCanonicalModel
- The canonical model is defined in /ripple-core/schema.ts
- No package may define its own schema for:
  - Decisions
  - Code structure
  - Conversations
  - Facts
  - Events
- Any new concept must be added to /ripple-core/schema.ts first

PACKAGE RESPONSIBILITIES

ripple-extension:
- captures raw conversation data only
- no interpretation or transformation of meaning

ripple-parser:
- converts codebases into deterministic structural graphs
- no semantic reasoning

ripple-memory:
- extracts reasoning, decisions, assumptions using LLMs
- outputs strictly validated canonical schema objects

ripple-mcp:
- read-only interface over canonical model
- no mutation or transformation logic

ripple-landing:
- UI only
- consumes aggregated snapshot data only

DATA FLOW RULE

All system data must converge into a single canonical state:

extension → parser → memory → ripple-core merger → canonical ProjectState → MCP + UI

NO LOCAL TRUTH RULE

- No package may define its own version of:
  - Decision
  - Fact
  - Node
  - Project state
- If it is not in /ripple-core/schema.ts, it does not exist

INTEGRATION RULE

- If a feature cannot be expressed in the canonical model, it must not be built
- Schema changes must precede implementation changes

ANTI-FRAGMENTATION RULE

- No duplicate representations of project state
- No parallel "memory systems"
- No independent truth layers per package

DEBUGGING RULE

- If system outputs are inconsistent, assume schema mismatch first
- Do not assume UI or pipeline bugs before validating canonical model alignment

SECURITY RULES

- Never hardcode secrets, keys, or tokens
- Use environment variables for any config that varies by environment
- Validate any external/user input
- Never expose internal errors to the UI — show safe messages, log details to console

CODE QUALITY RULES

- TypeScript strict mode always, never use any
- Every function has typed inputs and outputs
- Every error is caught and handled — nothing fails silently
- Name things clearly, no abbreviations
- Every function does one thing only
- Code must be readable by a beginner

COMMENTING RULES

- Every file has a comment at the top explaining its purpose in one sentence
- Every function has a comment above it explaining what it does in plain English
- Explain WHY decisions were made, not just what the code does
- Comments must be understandable by a beginner
- Never write obvious comments like "// loop through array"

MAINTAINABILITY RULES

- Delete dead code immediately
- If editing a file over 300 lines, evaluate splitting it first
- No TODO comments without a linked issue
- Optimize for future changeability over cleverness
- Prefer editing existing files over creating new ones
- Split any file over 200 lines
- Every new file has a single clearly stated purpose in a comment at the top
- If a file imports from more than two other modules, question whether it's doing too much
- Check /src/lib (or package-local shared utilities) before creating any utility
- Never duplicate logic

ANTI-OVERENGINEERING RULES

- Prefer simple code over reusable abstractions
- Do not abstract until duplication exists twice
- Build the simplest thing that works first, then refactor if needed
- If you cannot explain why an abstraction exists in one sentence, it should not exist
- No classes unless explicitly justified
- No generic abstractions without 2+ real use cases
- No manager, handler, or wrapper layers without clear justification
- Prefer explicit code over magical patterns

UI QUALITY RULES (ripple-landing)

- UI never contains business logic
- UI never makes direct API calls — consumes aggregated snapshot data only
- Never split headlines into individual word spans for animation — animate whole elements only
- Always confirm button variants have explicit background and text color classes applied
- After building any UI, reason through whether anything could overlap or clip before finishing
- Never use position absolute without confirming child elements cannot overflow their container
- Responsive check required after every UI build: does this work at 375px mobile and 1440px desktop
- If animation involves individual elements like spans or list items, confirm text flow is not broken by the split
- After any fix, run through this checklist:
  - Headline renders as one clean block
  - All buttons are visibly styled
  - Nothing overlaps or clips
  - All sections stack correctly on mobile
